}{TEXT -1 4 " , \n" }{HYPERLNK 17 "Summary on procedures" 1 "" "summar
y" }{TEXT -1 2 " ," }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {HYPERLNK 17 "comments in a proc
edure" 1 "" "comments" }{TEXT 275 1 " " }{TEXT -1 1 "," }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {HYPERLNK 17 "nargs" 1 "" "nargs" }{TEXT 271 2 " ," }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 260 "" 0 "" {TEXT 259 19 "Proced
ures in Maple" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 260 19 "proc .. end proc:  " }{TEXT -1 202 "A procedure is the m
ost general way that a function of many parameters can be defined in M
aple. These parameters may describe any Maple expression, function or \+
other procedure. The most basic syntax is:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 17 "> proc( par_seq )" }}{PARA 0 "
" 0 "" {TEXT -1 16 "        stat_seq" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 12 " \+
  end proc;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 162 "where  par_seq is
 a sequence of parameters on which the procedure depends and stat_seq \+
is a sequence of statements which is executed when the procedure is ca
lled." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 84 "
Example: The following simple procedure finds x^2+y^2  for the given p
arameters x,y." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 44 "SumSquares:=proc(x,y)\n   x^2+y^2;\nend proc;\n" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%+SumSquaresGf*6$%\"xG%\"yG6\"F)F),&*
$)9$\"\"#\"\"\"F/*$)9%F.F/F/F)F)F)" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "SumSquares(1,2);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#
\"\"&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 17 "SumSquares(a,2);\n
" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#,&*$)%\"aG\"\"#\"\"\"F(\"\"%F(" }}
}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "return" {TEXT 
261 6 "RETURN" }{TEXT -1 229 ":  In the last example the result x^2+y^
2 was automatically returned. In general, the last value computed is r
eturned unless there is an explicit RETURN(par_seq) statement where pa
r_seq is a sequence of parameters to be returned." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 101 "Example: The following p
rocedure, Member, tests a given parameter x for membership of a given \+
list a:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 1 " " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 130 "Member:=proc(x,a)\n    for v in a do\n        if v=x
 then\n           RETURN(true);\n        end if;\n    end do;\n    fal
se;\nend proc; " }}{PARA 7 "" 1 "" {TEXT -1 64 "Warning, `v` is implic
itly declared local to procedure `Member`\n" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#>%'MemberGf*6$%\"xG%\"aG6#%\"vG6\"F+C$?&8$9%%%trueG@$/F
.9$-%'RETURNG6#F0%&falseGF+F+F+" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 
48 "\nNotice the warning about the local variable v.\n" }}{PARA 0 "> \+
" 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "L:=[4,sin(y),Pi/3.0,-3.2];" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "
" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"LG7&\"\"%-%$sinG6#%\"yG,$*&$\"+LLLLL!#5\"\"\"%#Pi
GF0F0$!#K!\"\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 28 "Member(Pi
/3,L); Member(4,L);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#%&falseG" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#%%trueG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 2 "v;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#%\"vG" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "local" {TEXT 262 
13 "local, global" }{TEXT -1 635 ": In the last example, a new paramet
er v was introduced which was assigned within the do loop to the vario
us values of the given input list. However, on leaving the procedure, \+
v reverted to an unassigned variable. We may explicitly declare a new \+
parameter within a procedure to be local so that it is unassigned on l
eaving the procedure. An undeclared parameter appearing within a proce
dure is local by default and a warning message to that effect is print
ed when the procedure is first defined as in the example above. If the
 parameter assignment is to be preserved on leaving the procedure then
 the parameter is assigned as global.  " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 
"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 106 "Local and global parameter \+
declarations must take place immediately after the first line of the p
rocedure:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 
15 "> proc(par_seq)" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 22 "        local loc_
seq;" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 24 "        global glob_seq;" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 12 "           ." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 
12 "           ." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 12 "   end proc;" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 9 "Example: \+
" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 141 "Member:=proc(x,a)\n    local v;\n    for v in a do\n
        if v=x then\n           RETURN(true)\n        end if;\n    end
 do;\n    false;\nend proc;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%'Memb
erGf*6$%\"xG%\"aG6#%\"vG6\"F+C$?&8$9%%%trueG@$/F.9$-%'RETURNG6#F0%&fal
seGF+F+F+" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "Member(3,L);v;
" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#%&falseG" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#%\"vG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 
0 "" 0 "error" {TEXT 263 6 "ERROR:" }{TEXT -1 51 " Error message print
ing in procedures. The command " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 20 "ERROR(error message)" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 73 "which causes the procedur
e to terminate and the error message is printed." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 82 "Example: Test the second \+
argument of procedure Member to ensure that it is a list!" }}{PARA 0 "
" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 230 "Mem
ber:=proc(x,a)\n    local v;\n    if not type(a,list) then\n       ERR
OR(\"The 2nd argument is not a list!\")\n    end if;\n    for v in a d
o\n       if v = x then\n          RETURN(true)\n       end if;\n    e
nd do;\n    false;\nend proc:\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 33 "Member(4,\{4,sin(y),Pi/3.0,-3.2\});" }}{PARA 8 "" 1 "" {TEXT 
-1 51 "Error, (in Member) The 2nd argument is not a list!\n" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "type" {TEXT 264 
13 "Type Checking" }{TEXT -1 197 ":  An automatic checking of the type
 of any parameter inputted into a procedure can be defined. This is an
alogous to (but not exactly the same as) type declarations in  FORTRAN
 or C. The syntax is " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 
"" {TEXT -1 11 "name:: type" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 
0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 69 "for any parameter name in the procedure paramet
er sequence (procseq)." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 76 "Example: Instead of the explicit if statement above \+
in Member, define it by:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 148 "Member:=proc(x,a::list)\n    local
 v;\n    for v in a do\n        if v=x then\n           RETURN(true)\n
        end if;\n    end do;\n    false;\nend proc:\n" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "Member(4,\{4,5,3\});" }}{PARA 8 "" 
1 "" {TEXT -1 101 "Error, invalid input: Member expects its 2nd argume
nt, a, to be of type list, but received \{3, 4, 5\}\n" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "rem" {TEXT 265 15 "optio
n remember" }{TEXT -1 468 ":  There are a number of options that can b
e chosen in a procedure (see ?options for a full description). One of \+
the most useful options is remember.  This option ensures that any pre
viously computed or defined values of the given procedure are stored i
n a table for the current session. Then Maple looks up the table befor
e computing the procedure for the given parameter values. This option \+
is particularly useful for expensive computations or recurrence relati
ons. " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 136 "Example: Compute the Fibonacci sequence given by F(0)=0,
 F(1)=1 and  F(n)=F(n-1)+F(n-2). This can be computed directly by the \+
procedure:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 121 "F:=proc(n)\n    if (n=0) then\n       0;\n    elif (
n=1) then\n       1;\n    else\n       F(n-1)+F(n-2);\n    end if;\nen
d proc;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"FGf*6#%\"nG6\"F(F(@'/9$
\"\"!F,/F+\"\"\"F.,&-F$6#,&F+F.F.!\"\"F.-F$6#,&F+F.\"\"#F3F.F(F(F(" }}
}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 146 "
Notice that Maple will allow nested procedures where a procedure calls
 itself provided the nesting terminates after a finite number of such \+
calls!" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 17 "F(5),F(10),F(15);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6%\"\"&\"#b
\"$5'" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 319 "However this method for calculating Fibonaaci terms is v
ery inefficient since we are repeatedly computing the same terms over \+
again. You would not be able to compute F(100) by this method which wo
uld involve more that 10^20 additions! We may use the option remember \+
to store previously computed or defined values of F.\n" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 145 "Fib:= proc(n) \n   option remember
;\n   if (n=0) then\n      0;\n   elif (n=1) then\n      1;\n   else  \+
 \n      Fib(n-1)+Fib(n-2);\n   end if;\nend proc;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "
" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%$FibGf*6#%\"nG6\"6#%)rememberGF(@'/9$\"\"!F./F-\"\"
\"F0,&-F$6#,&F-F0F0!\"\"F0-F$6#,&F-F0\"\"#F5F0F(F(F(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 108 "With the remem
ber option selected we can now define Fib(0) and Fib(1) as required an
d then evaluate Fib(n).\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 
"Fib(20);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"%ln" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "Fib(100);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6
#\"6v]\">Ez\"[[Aa$" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 
"" 0 "summary" {TEXT 266 7 "Summary" }{TEXT -1 73 ": The most general \+
syntax for a procedure called procname is of the form:" }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 28 "> procname:= proc( p
ar_seq )" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 17 "   local loc_seq;" }}{PARA 0 
"" 0 "" {TEXT -1 19 "   global glob_seq;" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 
20 "   options opt_seq ;" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 11 "   stat_seq" 
}}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 12 "   end proc;" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 107 "par_seq is a sequence \+
of symbols separated by commas giving the parameters on which the proc
edure depends. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 76 "Type declarations of t
he form name::type  may be made within this sequence. " }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 96 "The local and global variables loc_seq and glob_seq \+
appear next followed by any options chosen. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 93 "Then stat_seq follows being a sequence of ordinary Maple statem
ents separated by semicolons. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 61 "Finally
 the procedure terminates with the end proc statement." }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "comments" {TEXT 269 23 "Com
ments in Procedures:" }{TEXT -1 53 "  A user comment can be introduced
 after a # symbol. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "
" {TEXT -1 9 "Example:\n" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 92 "Addnos:
=proc(x,y)\n    #This program adds two numbers\n    x+y; #This is the \+
answer\nend proc:\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "Addn
os(4,6);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"#5" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "
" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "nargs" {TEXT 270 56 "nargs parame
ter - Optional Arguments in Maple Procedures" }{TEXT -1 238 ": We have
 previously seen a number of Maple functions or procedures with option
al arguments e.g. plot, series, solve ,... One way to achieve this is \+
to use the nargs parameter which counts the number of arguments suppli
ed to a procedure. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "
" {TEXT -1 124 "As an example, consider the degree function which comp
utes the degree of any polynomial expression in one or more variables:
" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 25 
"x:='x': p:=x^4-5*x^2+x-1;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"pG,*
*$)%\"xG\"\"%\"\"\"F**&\"\"&F*)F(\"\"#F*!\"\"F(F*F*F/" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "degree(p);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#\"\"%" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 31 "q:=
4*x^3*y^2-6*x*y^2+x^2+2*x*y;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"qG
,**(\"\"%\"\"\")%\"xG\"\"$F()%\"yG\"\"#F(F(*(\"\"'F(F*F(F,F(!\"\"*$)F*
F.F(F(*(F.F(F*F(F-F(F(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "d
egree(q);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"\"&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "degree(q,x);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 
20 "6#\"\"$" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "degree(q,y);
" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"\"#" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "degree(q,\{x,y\});" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 
"6#\"\"&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 208 "These last few examples illustrate the optional second a
rgument which allows us to determine the degree of a particular variab
le or set of variables. Type ?degree to find out the full nature of th
is command.\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 376 "The following p
rocedure illustrates the way in which optional arguments can be introd
uced with a user defined procedure called DEGREE which has a second op
tional argument which can be absent, a variable name or a set of such \+
names. This procedure computes the degree of any polynomial expression
 and illustrates a variety of Maple commands and structures that we ha
ve studied." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 1553 "\nDEGREE:=
proc(A::algebraic,x::\{name,set(name)\})\n#\n# A is the algebraic expr
ession\n# x is a variable name or set of such names\n#\n    local s,t;
\n# nargs is a Maple variable which counts \n# the no of arguments of \+
the procedure\n#\n    if nargs = 1 then # no optional argument given \+
\n#\n#\n# indets is a Maple function giving the \n# set of unknown var
iables on which its \n# argument depends\n#\n        s:=indets(A); # s
 is the set of unknowns of A\n        if not type(s,set(name)) then \n
           ERROR(`input not polynomial`);\n        end if;\n        DE
GREE(A,s); # call procedure with 2nd arg s\n    elif type(A, constant)
 then \n        0\n    elif type(A,name) then # A a single unknown var
iable\n        if type(x,name) then # x a single unknown variable\n   \+
         if A=x then \n                1 \n            else \n        \+
        0 \n            end if; # A=x exactly\n        else \n        \+
    if member(A,x) then 1 else 0 end if;\n        end if;\n    elif ty
pe(A,`+`) then  \n# A is a sum of terms and find max degree term\n# A \+
is a sum of terms and find max degree term\n        max(seq(DEGREE(t,x
),t=A));\n# this is the max of seq indexed by operands of A\n    elif \+
type(A,`*`) then  \n# A is a product of terms whose degrees are added
\n        max(seq(DEGREE(t,x),t=A));\n        s:=0;\n# this is the max
 of seq indexed by operands of A\n        for t in A do\n            s
:=s+DEGREE(t,x);\n        end do;\n        s;\n    elif type(A, algebr
aic^integer) then\n        DEGREE(op(1,A),x)*op(2,A);\n    else \n    \+
    ERROR(`cannot compute degree`);\n    end if;\nend proc:\n" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "DEGREE(p);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 
"" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"\"%" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "
DEGREE(q,\{x,y\});" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"\"&" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 74 "\nThe procedure also computed the \+
degree of integer powers of polynomials.\n" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "DEGREE(q^3,x);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"
\"*" }}}}{MARK "3 0" 0 }{VIEWOPTS 1 1 0 1 1 1803 1 1 1 1 }
{PAGENUMBERS 0 1 2 33 1 1 }
